In .NET 10 (MAUI), `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` was deprecated/removed because Android itself deprecated `SOFT_INPUT_ADJUST_RESIZE`. The recommended approach is now **edge-to-edge display with `WindowInsets` handling**.

## Solution

In your `Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs`:

```csharp
using Android.Views;
using AndroidX.Core.View;
using Microsoft.Maui;

public class MainActivity : MauiAppCompatActivity
{
    protected override void OnCreate(Android.OS.Bundle? savedInstanceState)
    {
        base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);

        // Enable edge-to-edge
        WindowCompat.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(Window!, false);

        // Listen for inset changes (keyboard open/close)
        ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(Window!.DecorView, new InsetsListener());
    }
}

class InsetsListener : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
{
    public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(Android.Views.View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
    {
        var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
        var systemBars = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.SystemBars());

        // Pad the bottom by whichever is larger: keyboard or nav bar
        v.SetPadding(0, systemBars.Top, 0, Math.Max(imeInsets.Bottom, systemBars.Bottom));

        return insets;
    }
}
```

## Key points

- **`SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(false)`** opts into edge-to-edge, letting your app draw behind system bars.
- **`WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime()`** gives the keyboard height; you apply it as bottom padding so your content (including the input bar) shifts up.
- This replaces the old `SoftInput = SoftInput.AdjustResize` / `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` pattern that Android deprecated in API 30+.

If you also use a `ScrollView` or `CollectionView` for messages, the padding change will naturally push them up with the input bar—no extra XAML changes needed.
